  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 915 PM EDT Thursday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...The Flood Watch has been cancelled. While the \nheavy rain threat has greatly diminished, scattered showers will\nremain possible overnight over northeast North Carolina. Otherwise,\ngradually cooler and more comfortable overnight.\n\nLatest analysis reveals the slow-moving cold front is now \nwell south of the area, extending from just north of Hatteras,\nacross the NC/SC border into N GA/N AL. Aloft, a mid-level\ntrough remains just offshore of the east coast, with W-SW flow\naloft providing a feed of Gulf moisture from the western Gulf\ncoast, near to now-TD Bertha continues. Fortunately for our \nlocal area, showers remain far more numerous along and south of \nthe front, with cooler, more stable regime post-frontal allowing\nfor isolated to scattered showers where PWs AOA 2\" remain over \nfar SE VA and northeast NC. For the rest of the area, drier air \ncontinues to filter into the region. dewpoints generally in the \nlow 60s north of Richmond (with mid to upper 50s just N of our \nlocal area). Have allowed for lingering showers overnight across\nthe southeast CWA, but with the heavy rain threat greatly \ndiminished, have dropped the Flood Watch for the remaining \nportion of our area. Overnight lows will be cool and noticeably \nless humid across the northern half of the area. Look for \novernight lows in the upper 50s to low 60s north, mid to upper \n60s farther south and east.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Below average temperatures are expected \ntomorrow through the weekend, before trending back to seasonal \nfor late July early next week. There are daily chances for \nafternoon storms, but overall drier than the current wet \npattern.\n\nThe cold front will remain south of the area through the weekend, \ndeveloping a weak low along the front over central North Carolina. \nResidual moisture may bring more showers to SE portions of the area \nas the low pushes additional chances into the area. The FA will then \nbe positioned under an UL trough over the weekend with onshore flow \nin place at the sfc. This will lead to cooler, below-average \ntemperatures for a few days. Highs on Fri and Sat will be in the \nupper 70s to around 80. Warming up slightly for Sunday with highs in \nthe low to mid 80s. Near-average temps return early next week. \nLooking to be overall a dry weekend, but a few isolated \nshowers/storms cannot be ruled out during the afternoons.",
